# todo/t0953 — a closure LITERAL at a call-argument position mints a heap
# environment that is never freed after the call returns.
#
# Two cells, because the class is WIDER than the characterisation
# `tests/sanitize/LEAK_ALLOWLIST.txt` inherited for it ("every builtin HOF call
# (map/filter/fold) leaks an 8-byte env"):
#
#   A  builtin Vector HOF   `nums.map((int n): n + base)`
#   B  a plain USER function taking `Callable[int(int)]`
#
# B is the discriminator: it has no builtin HOF anywhere, and it leaks the same
# frame. So the write site is the closure-literal ARGUMENT, not the HOF.
#
# HEAP-FORCED: both closures CAPTURE `base`, so each env is a real 16-byte malloc
# rather than the 8-byte placeholder a non-capturing closure gets.
#
# INTENDED: prints 41 and 41, and is ASan-clean under `detect_leaks=1`, on C and
# LLVM. At HEAD it prints both correctly and leaks 32 bytes in 2 allocations,
# both `__gorget_closure_env_alloc`. Stdout-invisible, so this is wired to
# `assert_gg_sanitize_clean`.

int apply(Callable[int(int)] f, int x):
    return f(x)

void main():
    int base = 40
    Vector[int] nums = [1, 2, 3]

    # A — builtin HOF.
    auto out = nums.map((int n): n + base)
    print(f"{out.get(0).unwrap()}")

    # B — a plain user function. No HOF, same leak.
    print(f"{apply((int n): n + base, 1)}")
